::GEO_U02_L02_GP_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L02_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which transformation preserves distance? {
= Translation#Correct. A translation is rigid and keeps all distances the same.
~ Dilation with scale factor 2#This doubles distances, so distance is not preserved.
~ Vertical stretch#A stretch changes some distances.
~ Enlargement#An enlargement changes size, so distances are not preserved.
}

::GEO_U02_L02_GP_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L02_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.2<br><br>Question:<br>Segment AB has length 6, and its image A'B' has length 6. What does this evidence show? {
~ Distance changed#The two lengths are equal, so this evidence does not show a change.
= Distance was preserved for this segment#Correct. The corresponding segment length stayed 6.
~ The image must be larger#Equal lengths do not show enlargement.
~ The transformation must be a dilation with scale factor 2#A scale factor of 2 would change length 6 to 12.
}

::GEO_U02_L02_GP_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L02_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which transformation does not preserve distance when the scale factor is 3? {
~ Reflection#A reflection is rigid and preserves distance.
~ Rotation#A rotation is rigid and preserves distance.
= Dilation#Correct. A dilation with scale factor 3 changes lengths by a factor of 3.
~ Translation#A translation is rigid and preserves distance.
}

::GEO_U02_L02_GP_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L02_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.2<br><br>Question:<br>A triangle is reflected across the y-axis. Which statement is true? {
~ The side lengths double#Reflection does not change size.
~ The side lengths are cut in half#Reflection does not shrink the figure.
~ The triangle is not congruent to its image#A reflection preserves distance, so the image is congruent.
= The triangle is congruent to its image#Correct. Reflections preserve distance and angle measure.
}

::GEO_U02_L02_GP_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L02_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which evidence best shows that a transformation preserves distance? {
~ The image is in a different quadrant#Location alone does not prove distances stayed the same.
~ The image looks similar to the preimage#Appearance is not enough; corresponding distances must be compared.
= Each corresponding side length is equal#Correct. Equal corresponding distances show distance preservation.
~ The image has different side lengths#Different side lengths show distance was not preserved.
}
